\STATE \textbf{K-MEANS}($\boldsymbol{x}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}_n, k$)
\STATE \quad initialize cluster centers $\mu_1 = x_{i_1}, \ldots, \mu_k = x_{i_k}$ (e.g.\ randomly)
\REPEAT
    \STATE \quad classify $\boldsymbol{x}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}_n$ to each's nearest $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$
    \STATE \quad recalculate $\boldsymbol{\mu}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{\mu}_k$
\UNTIL{no change in $\boldsymbol{\mu}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{\mu}_k$}
\STATE \textbf{Return} $(\boldsymbol{\mu}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{\mu}_k)$
\end{algorithmic}

The calculation of the cluster midpoint $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ for points $\boldsymbol{x}_1, \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}_l$ is done by
\[
\boldsymbol{\mu} = \frac{1}{l} \sum_{i=1}^{l} x_i
\]

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[max width=\textwidth]{2025_05_12_af19cd5e0d1b8a465ffeg-232}
  \caption{K-means with two classes ($k=2$) applied to 30 data points. Far left is the dataset with the initial centers, and to the right is the cluster after each iteration. After three iterations convergence is reached.}
\end{figure}

While this algorithm does not guarantee convergence, it usually converges very quickly. This means that the number of iteration steps is typically much smaller than the number of data points. Its complexity is $O(ndkt)$, where $n$ is the total number of points, $d$ the dimensionality of the feature space, and $t$ the number of iteration steps.

