``GoalReached'' calculates whether the argument is a goal node, and ``Successors'' calculates the list of all successor nodes of its argument. Figure 6.7 shows a snapshot of breadth-first search.

\textbf{Analysis} Since breadth-first search completely searches through every depth and reaches every depth in finite time, it is complete if the branching factor $b$ is finite. The optimal (that is, the shortest) solution is found if the costs of all actions are the same (see Exercise 6.7 on page 123). Computation time and memory space grow exponentially with the depth of the tree. For a tree with constant branching factor $b$ and depth $d$, the total compute time is thus given by
\[
c = \sum_{i=0}^{d} b^i = \frac{b^{d+1} - 1}{b - 1} = O(b^d).
\]
Although only the last level is saved in memory, the memory space requirement is also $O(b^d)$.

\textbf{BREADTH-FIRST-SEARCH(NodeList, Goal)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item NewNodes $\gets$ ;
    \item \textbf{For all} Node $\in$ NodeList
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{If} GoalReached(Node, Goal)
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Return} (``Solution found'', Node)
        \end{itemize}
        \item NewNodes $\gets$ Append(NewNodes, Successors(Node))
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{If} NewNodes
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Return} (BREADTH-FIRST-SEARCH(NewNodes, Goal))
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Else}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Return} (``No solution'')
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

