\begin{definition}
An atomic formula $p(t_1, \ldots, t_n)$ is true (or valid) under the interpretation $\mathbb{I}$ if, after interpretation and evaluation of all terms $t_1, \ldots, t_n$ and interpretation of the predicate $p$ through the $n$-place relation $r$, it holds that
$$
\left(\mathbb{I}\left(t_{1}\right), \ldots, \mathbb{I}\left(t_{n}\right)\right) \in r .
$$
\end{definition}

\begin{itemize}
  \item The truth of quantifierless formulas follows from the truth of atomic formulas—as in propositional calculus—through the semantics of the logical operators defined in Table 2.1 on page 25.
