This general method of explicitly caching (or \textit{tabling}) results from recursive calls to avoid recomputation provides a simple way to get \textit{most} of the benefits of full dynamic programming. It is thus worth a careful look. In principle, such caching can be employed on any recursive algorithm. However, storing partial results would have done absolutely no good for such recursive algorithms as \textit{quicksort}, \textit{backtracking}, and \textit{depth-first search} because all the recursive calls made in these algorithms have distinct \textit{parameter values}. It doesn't pay to store something you will use once and never refer to again.

Caching makes sense only when the space of distinct parameter values is modest enough that we can afford the cost of storage. Since the argument to the recursive function fib\_c$(k)$ is an integer between 0 and $n$, there are only $O(n)$ values to cache. A linear amount of space for an exponential amount of time is an excellent tradeoff. But as we shall see, we can do even better by eliminating the recursion completely.

\textit{Take-Home Lesson:} Explicit caching of the results of recursive calls provides \textit{most} of the benefits of dynamic programming, usually including the same running time as the more elegant full solution. If you prefer doing extra programming to more subtle thinking, I guess you can stop here.

\subsection{Fibonacci Numbers by Dynamic Programming}

We can calculate $F_{n}$ in linear time more easily by explicitly specifying the order of evaluation of the recurrence relation:

\begin{verbatim}
long fib_dp(int n) {
    int i; /* counter */
    long f[MAXN+1]; /* array for caching values */
    f[0] = 0;
    f[1] = 1;
    for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2];
    }
    return(f[n]);
}
\end{verbatim}

Observe that we have removed all recursive calls! We evaluate the Fibonacci numbers from smallest to biggest and store all the results, so we know that we have $F_{i-1}$ and $F_{i-2}$ ready whenever we need to compute $F_{i}$. The linearity of this algorithm is now obvious. Each of the $n$ values is simply computed as the sum of two integers, in $O(n)$ total time and space.

