The key to using \textit{qsort} is realizing what its arguments do. It sorts the first \textit{nel} elements of an array (pointed to by \textit{base}), where each element is \textit{width}-bytes long. We can thus sort arrays of 1-byte characters, 4-byte integers, or 100-byte records, all by changing the value of \textit{width}.

The desired output order is determined by the \textit{compare} function. It takes as arguments pointers to two \textit{width}-byte elements, and returns a negative number if the first belongs before the second in sorted order, a positive number if the second belongs before the first, or zero if they are the same. Here is a comparison function to sort integers in increasing order:

\begin{verbatim}
int intcompare(int *i, int *j)
{
    if (*i > *j) return (1);
    if (*i < *j) return (-1);
    return (0);
}
\end{verbatim}

This comparison function can be used to sort an array \textit{a}, of which the first $n$ elements are occupied, as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
qsort(a, n, sizeof(int), intcompare);
\end{verbatim}

The name \textit{qsort} suggests that quicksort is the algorithm implemented in this library function, although this is usually irrelevant to the user.

