Such bottleneck spanning trees have interesting applications when the edge weights are interpreted as costs, capacities, or strengths. A less efficient but conceptually simpler way to solve such problems might be to delete all ``heavy'' edges from the graph and ask whether the result is still connected. These kinds of tests can be done with BFS or DFS.

The minimum spanning tree of a graph is unique if all \textit{m} edge weights in the graph are distinct. Otherwise the order in which Prim’s/Kruskal’s algorithm breaks ties determines which minimum spanning tree is returned.

There are two important variants of a minimum spanning tree that are \textit{not} solvable with the techniques presented in this section:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Steiner tree} -- Suppose we want to wire a bunch of houses together, but have the freedom to add extra intermediate vertices to serve as a shared junction. This problem is known as a \textit{minimum Steiner tree}, and is discussed in the catalog in Section 19.10.
