Our final decision concerns the \textit{possible values} we allow for each square. We have two possibilities:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Local count} -- Our backtrack search works correctly if the routine that generates candidates for board position $(i, j)$ (possible values) does the obvious thing and allows all digits 1 to 9 that have not appeared in the given row, column, or sector.
    \item \textit{Look ahead} -- But what if our current partial solution has some \textit{other} open square where there are no candidates remaining under the local count criteria? There is no possible way to complete this partial solution into a full Sudoku grid. Thus, there \textit{really} are zero possible moves to consider for $(i, j)$ because of what is happening elsewhere on the board!
\end{itemize}

We will discover this obstruction eventually, when we pick this square for expansion, discover it has no moves, and then have to backtrack. But why wait, since all our efforts until then will be wasted? We are \textit{much} better off backtracking immediately and moving on.\footnote{This look-ahead condition might have naturally followed from the most-constrained square selection, had it been permitted to select squares with no moves. However, my implementation credited squares that already contained digits as having no moves, thus limiting the next square choices to squares with at least one move.}

Successful pruning requires looking ahead to see when a partial solution is doomed to go nowhere, and backing off as soon as possible.

