\subsection{Knapsack Problem}

\textbf{Input description}: A set of items $S = \{1, \ldots, n\}$, where item $i$ has size $s_{i}$ and value $v_{i}$. A knapsack capacity $C$.

\textbf{Problem description}: Find the subset $S'$ of $S$ that maximizes the value of $\sum_{i \in S'} v_{i}$, given $\sum_{i \in S'} s_{i} \leq C$; that is, all the items fit in a knapsack of size $C$.

\textbf{Discussion}: The knapsack problem arises in resource allocation with financial constraints. How do you select what things to buy given a fixed budget? Everything has a cost and value, so we seek the most value for the given cost. The \textit{knapsack problem} should invoke the image of the backpacker who is constrained by a fixed-size knapsack, and so must fill it with the most useful and portable items.

The most common formulation is the \textit{0/1 knapsack problem}, where each item must either be placed entirely in the knapsack or discarded. Objects cannot be broken up arbitrarily, so it is not fair taking one can of Coke from a six-pack or opening a can to take just a sip. It is this 0/\textit{1} property that makes the knapsack problem hard, for a simple greedy algorithm finds the optimal selection when we are allowed to subdivide objects. We compute the ``price per pound'' for each item, and repeatedly take the most expensive item or the biggest part thereof until the knapsack is full. Unfortunately, this 0/\textit{1} constraint is inherent in most applications.

Issues that arise in selecting the best algorithm include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Does every item have the same cost, or perhaps the same size?} -- When all items are worth exactly the same, we maximize our total value by taking the greatest number of items. Therefore, the optimal solution is to sort the items in order of increasing size and insert them into the knapsack in this order until no more fit. The problem is similarly solved when all objects
\end{itemize}

