\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Weighted vs. unweighted} – Each edge (or vertex) in a \textit{weighted} graph $G$ is assigned a numerical value, or weight. The edges of a road network graph might be weighted with their length, drive time, or speed limit, depending upon the application. In \textit{unweighted} graphs, there is no cost distinction between various edges and vertices.
\end{itemize}

The difference between weighted and unweighted graphs becomes particularly apparent in finding the shortest path between two vertices. For unweighted graphs, a shortest path is one that has the fewest number of edges, and can be found using a breadth-first search (BFS) as discussed in this chapter. Shortest paths in weighted graphs requires more sophisticated algorithms, as discussed in Chapter 8.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Simple vs. non-simple} – Certain types of edges complicate the task of working with graphs. A \textit{self-loop} is an edge ($x, x$) involving only one vertex. An edge ($x, y$) is a \textit{multiedge} if it occurs more than once in the graph.\\
Both of these structures require special care in implementing graph algorithms. Hence any graph that avoids them is called \textit{simple}. I confess that all implementations in this book are designed to work \textbf{only} on simple graphs.
  \item \textit{Sparse vs. dense}: Graphs are \textit{sparse} when only a small fraction of the possible vertex pairs actually have edges defined between them. Graphs where a large fraction of the vertex pairs define edges are called \textit{dense}. A graph is \textit{complete} if it contains all possible edges; for a simple undirected graph on $n$ vertices that is $\binom{n}{2} = \left(n^{2}-n\right)/2$ edges. There is no official boundary between what is called sparse and what is called dense, but dense graphs typically have $\Theta\left(n^{2}\right)$ edges, while sparse graphs are linear in size.
\end{itemize}

Sparse graphs are usually sparse for application-specific reasons. Road networks must be sparse because of the complexity of road junctions. The ghastliest intersection I have ever managed to identify is the endpoint of just seven different roads. Junctions of electrical components are similarly limited to the number of wires that can meet at a point, perhaps except for power and ground.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Cyclic vs. acyclic} – A \textit{cycle} is a closed path of 3 or more vertices that has no repeating vertices except the start/end point. An \textit{acyclic} graph does not contain any cycles. \textit{Trees} are undirected graphs that are connected and acyclic. They are the simplest interesting graphs. Trees are inherently recursive structures, because cutting any edge leaves two smaller trees.
\end{itemize}

