\textit{2.7. LOGARITHMS AND THEIR APPLICATIONS}

Exponential functions grow at a distressingly fast rate, as anyone who has ever tried to pay off a credit card balance understands. Thus, inverse exponential functions (logarithms) grow refreshingly slowly. Logarithms arise in any process where things are repeatedly halved. We’ll now look at several examples.

\subsection{Logarithms and Binary Search}
Binary search is a good example of an $O(\log n)$ algorithm. To locate a particular person $p$ in a telephone book $2^{2}$ containing $n$ names, you start by comparing $p$ against the middle, or ($n/2$)nd name, say \textit{Monroe, Marilyn}. Regardless of whether $p$ belongs before this middle name (\textit{Dean, James}) or after it (\textit{Presley, Elvis}), after just one comparison you can discard one half of all the names in the book. The number of steps the algorithm takes equals the number of times we can halve $n$ until only one name is left. By definition, this is exactly $\log_{2} n$. Thus, twenty comparisons suffice to find any name in the million-name Manhattan phone book!

Binary search is one of the most powerful ideas in algorithm design. This power becomes apparent if we imagine trying to find a name in an unsorted telephone book.

\subsection{Logarithms and Trees}
A binary tree of height 1 can have up to 2 leaf nodes, while a tree of height 2 can have up to 4 leaves. What is the height $h$ of a rooted binary tree with $n$ leaf nodes? Note that the number of leaves doubles every time we increase the height by 1. To account for $n$ leaves, $n=2^{h}$, which implies that $h=\log_{2} n$.

What if we generalize to trees that have $d$ children, where $d=2$ for the case of binary trees? A tree of height 1 can have up to $d$ leaf nodes, while one of height 2 can have up to $d^{2}$ leaves. The number of possible leaves multiplies by $d$ every time we increase the height by 1, so to account for $n$ leaves, $n=d^{h}$, which implies that $h=\log_{d} n$, as shown in Figure 2.7\footnotetext{If necessary, ask your grandmother what telephone books were.}

The punch line here is that short trees can have very many leaves, which is the main reason why binary trees prove fundamental to the design of fast data structures.

