scheduling requires sorting the jobs by importance, and then processing them in this sorted order.

The \textit{priority queue} is an abstract data type that provides more flexibility than simple sorting, because it allows new elements to enter a system at arbitrary intervals. It can be much more cost-effective to insert a new job into a priority queue than to re-sort everything on each such arrival.

The basic priority queue supports three primary operations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Insert}($Q, x$) -- Given item $x$, insert it into the priority queue $Q$.
  \item \textit{Find-Minimum}($Q$) or \textit{Find-Maximum}($Q$) -- Return a pointer to the item whose key value is smallest (or largest) among all keys in priority queue $Q$.
  \item \textit{Delete-Minimum}($Q$) or \textit{Delete-Maximum}($Q$) -- Remove the item whose key value is minimum (or maximum) from priority queue $Q$.
\end{itemize}

Naturally occurring processes are often informally modeled by priority queues. Single people maintain a priority queue of potential dating candidates, mentally if not explicitly. One's impression on meeting a new person maps directly to an attractiveness or desirability score, which serves as the \textit{key} field for inserting this new entry into the ``little black book'' priority queue data structure. Dating is the process of extracting the most desirable person from the data structure (\textit{Find-Maximum}), spending an evening to evaluate them better, and then reinserting them into the priority queue with a possibly revised score.

\textit{Take-Home Lesson:} Building algorithms around data structures such as dictionaries and priority queues leads to both clean structure and good performance.

