\subsection{Advanced Analysis (*)}
Ideally, each of us would be fluent in working with the mathematical techniques of asymptotic analysis. And ideally, each of us would be rich and good looking as well.

In this section I will survey the major techniques and functions employed in advanced algorithm analysis. I consider this optional material—it will not be used elsewhere in the textbook section of this book. That said, it will make some of the complexity functions reported in the Hitchhiker's Guide a little less mysterious.

\subsection{Esoteric Functions}
The bread-and-butter classes of complexity functions were presented in Section 2.3.1 (page 38). More esoteric functions also make appearances in advanced algorithm analysis. Although we will not see them much in this book, it is still good business to know what they mean and where they come from.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Inverse Ackermann's function} $\mathit{f}(n) = \mathit{\alpha}(n)$: This function arises in the detailed analysis of several algorithms, most notably the Union-Find data structure discussed in Section 8.1.3 (page 250). It is sufficient to think of this as geek talk for the slowest growing complexity function. Unlike the constant function $\mathit{f}(n) = 1$, $\mathit{\alpha}(n)$ eventually gets to infinity as $\mathit{n} \rightarrow \infty$, but it certainly takes its time about it. The value of $\mathit{\alpha}(n)$ is smaller than 5 for any value of $\mathit{n}$ that can be written in this physical universe.
  \item $\mathit{f}(n) = \log \log \mathit{n}$: The "log log" function is just that—the logarithm of the logarithm of $\mathit{n}$. One natural example of how it might arise is doing a binary search on a sorted array of only $\lg \mathit{n}$ items.
  \item $\mathit{f}(n) = \frac{\log \mathit{n}}{\log \log \mathit{n}}$: This function grows a little slower than $\log \mathit{n}$, because it is divided by an even slower growing function. To see where this arises, consider an $\mathit{n}$-leaf rooted tree of degree $\mathit{d}$. For binary trees, that is, when $\mathit{d} = 2$, the height $\mathit{h}$ is given
\end{itemize}

\[
\mathit{n} = 2^{\mathit{h}} \rightarrow \mathit{h} = \lg \mathit{n}
\]

by taking the logarithm of both sides of the equation. Now consider the height of such a tree when the degree $\mathit{d} = \log \mathit{n}$. Then

\[
\mathit{n} = (\log \mathit{n})^{\mathit{h}} \rightarrow \mathit{h} = \frac{\log \mathit{n}}{\log \log \mathit{n}}
\]

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathit{f}(n) = \log^{2} \mathit{n}$: This is the product of two log functions, $(\log \mathit{n}) \times (\log \mathit{n})$. It might arise if we wanted to count the bits looked at when doing a binary search on $\mathit{n}$ items, each of which was an integer from 1 to (say) $\mathit{n}^{2}$. Each such integer requires a $\lg(\mathit{n}^{2}) = 2 \lg \mathit{n}$ bit representation, and we look at $\lg \mathit{n}$ of them, for a total of $2 \lg^{2} \mathit{n}$ bits.
\end{itemize}

