\section{Stop and Think: Separate the Text}

\textbf{Stop and Think: Separate the Text}

\textit{Problem:} We need a way to separate the lines of text in the optical character-recognition system that we are building. Although there is some white space between the lines, problems like noise and the tilt of the page make it hard to find. How can we do line segmentation?

\textit{Solution:} Consider the following graph formulation. Treat each pixel in the image as a vertex in the graph, with an edge between two neighboring pixels. The weight of this edge should be proportional to how dark the pixels are. A segmentation between two lines is a path in this graph from the left to right side of the page. We seek a relatively straight path that avoids as much blackness as possible. This suggests that the \textit{shortest path} in the pixel graph will likely find a good line segmentation.

\textit{Take-Home Lesson:} Designing novel graph algorithms is very hard, so don't do it. Instead, try to design graphs that enable you to use classical algorithms to model your problem.

