\subsection{Distribution Sort: Sorting via Bucketing}
To sort names for a class roster or the telephone book, we could first partition them according to the first letter of the last name. This will create twenty-six different piles, or buckets, of names. Observe that any name in the \textit{J} pile must occur after all names in the \textit{I} pile, and before any name in the \textit{K} pile. Therefore, we can proceed to sort each pile individually and just concatenate the sorted piles together at the end.

Assuming the names are distributed evenly among the buckets, the resulting twenty-six sorting problems should each be substantially smaller than the original problem. By now further partitioning each pile based on the \textit{second} letter of each name, we can generate smaller and smaller piles. The set of names will be completely sorted as soon as every bucket contains only a single name. Such an algorithm is commonly called \textit{bucketsort} or \textit{distribution sort}.

Bucketing is a very effective idea whenever we are confident that the distribution of data will be roughly uniform. It is the idea that underlies hash tables, \textit{kd}-trees, and a variety of other practical data structures. The downside of such techniques is that the performance can be terrible when the data distribution is not what we expected. Although data structures such as balanced binary trees offer guaranteed worst-case behavior for any input distribution, no such promise exists for heuristic data structures on unexpected input distributions.

Non-uniform distributions do occur in real life. Consider Americans with the uncommon last name of Shifflett. When last I looked, the Manhattan telephone directory (with over one million names) contained exactly five Shiffletts. So how many Shiffletts should there be in a small city of 50,000 people? Figure 4.8 shows a small portion of the \textit{two and a half pages} of Shiffletts in the Charlottesville, Virginia telephone book. The Shifflett clan is a fixture of the region, but it would play havoc with any distribution sort program, as refining buckets from \textit{S} to \textit{Sh} to \textit{Shi} to \textit{Shif} to \textit{. . .} to \textit{Shifflett} results in no significant partitioning.

