\textit{18.9. NETWORK FLOW}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Augmenting path methods} – These algorithms repeatedly find a path of positive capacity from source to sink and add it to the flow. It can be shown that the flow through a network is optimal if and only if it contains no augmenting path. Since each augmentation adds something to the flow, we eventually reach the maximum. Network-flow algorithms differ in \textit{how} they select the augmenting path. If we are not careful, each augmenting path might add only a little bit to the total flow, and so the algorithm might take a long time to converge.
\item \textit{Preflow-push methods} – These algorithms push flows from one vertex to another, initially ignoring the constraint that in-flow must equal out-flow at each vertex. Preflow-push methods prove faster than augmenting-path methods, essentially because multiple paths can be augmented simultaneously. These algorithms are the method of choice and are implemented in the best codes described below.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Implementations}: High-performance codes for both maximum flow and minimum cost flow were developed by Andrew Goldberg and his collaborators. The codes HIPR and PRF~\cite{CG94} are provided for maximum flow, with the proviso that HIPR is recommended in most cases. For minimum-cost flow, the code of choice is CS~\cite{Gol97}. Both are written in C and available for non-commercial use from \url{http://www.avglab.com/andrew/soft.html}.

The C++ Boost Graph Library~\cite{SLL02} (\url{http://www.boost.org/libs/graph}) and Java JGraphT (\url{https://jgrapht.org}) both provide implementations of several network flow algorithms.

The First DIMACS Implementation Challenge on Network Flows and Matching~\cite{JM93} collected several implementations and generators for network flow, which are accessible from \url{http://dimacs.rutgers.edu/Challenges}. These include: (1) a preflow-push network flow implementation in C by Edward Rothberg, and (2) an implementation of eleven network flow variants in C, including the older Dinic and Karzanov algorithms by Anderson and Setubal.

\textbf{Notes}: Excellent books on network flows and its applications include~\cite{AMO93,Wil19}, with Goldberg and Tarjan~\cite{GT14} a short but nice expository survey. The fundamental maximum-flow, minimum-cut theorem is due to Ford and Fulkerson~\cite{FF62}. Expositions on the hardness of multicommodity flow~\cite{Ita78} include~\cite{Eve11}.

Conventional wisdom has long held that network flow should be computable in $\mathcal{O}(nm)$ time, finally achieved by Orlin~\cite{Orl13}. See~\cite{AMO93} for a history of algorithms for the problem. Empirical studies of flow algorithms include~\cite{GKK74,Gol97}.

Information flows through a network can be modeled as multicommodity flows, with the observation that replicating information at internal nodes can eliminate the need for distinct source-to-sink paths when multiple sinks are interested in the same information. The field of \textit{network coding}~\cite{YLCZ05} uses such ideas to achieve information flows at the theoretical limits of the max-flow, min-cut theorem.

\textbf{Related problems}: Linear programming (see page~482), matching (see page~562), connectivity (see page~568).

