We sought to identify all the strings of length $2k$ that are possible substrings of an unknown string \textit{S}, given the set of all length-$k$ substrings of \textit{S}. For example, suppose we know that \textit{AC}, \textit{CA}, and \textit{CC} are the only length-2 substrings of \textit{S}. It is possible that \textit{ACCA} is a substring of \textit{S}, since the center substring is one of our possibilities. However, \textit{CAAC} cannot be a substring of \textit{S}, since \textit{AA} is not a substring of \textit{S}. We needed to find a fast algorithm to construct all the consistent length-$2k$ strings, since \textit{S} could be very long.

The simplest algorithm to build the $2k$ strings would be to concatenate all $O(n^{2})$ pairs of $k$-strings together, and then test to make sure that all $(k-1)$ length-$k$ substrings spanning the boundary of the concatenation were in fact substrings, as shown in Figure 3.12. For example, the nine possible concatenations of \textit{AC}, \textit{CA}, and \textit{CC} are \textit{ACAC}, \textit{ACCA}, \textit{ACCC}, \textit{CAAC}, \textit{CACA}, \textit{CACC}, \textit{CCAC}, \textit{CCCA}, and \textit{CCCC}. Only \textit{CAAC} can be eliminated, because of the absence of \textit{AA} as a substring of \textit{S}.

