\textit{17.7. GENERATING GRAPHS}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Do I want directed or undirected graphs?} -- Most natural generation algorithms generate undirected graphs. These can be turned into directed graphs by flipping coins to orient the edges. Any graph can be oriented to be directed and acyclic (i.e., a DAG) by randomly permuting the vertices on a line and aiming each edge from left to right. With all such ideas, careful thought must be given to decide whether you are generating all graphs uniformly at random, and how much this matters to you.
\end{itemize}

You also must define what you mean by random. There are three primary models of random graphs, all of which generate graphs according to different probability distributions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Random edge generation} -- The Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi model is parameterized by a given edge probability \textit{p}. In this model, a coin is flipped for each pair of vertices \textit{x} and \textit{y} to decide whether to add an edge $(x, y)$. All \textit{labeled} graphs will be generated with equal probability when \textit{p} $= 1/2$, although smaller values of \textit{p} can be used to construct sparser random graphs.
\item \textit{Random edge selection} -- The second model is parameterized by the desired number of edges \textit{m}. It selects \textit{m} distinct edges uniformly at random. One way to do this is by drawing random $(x, y)$-pairs and creating an edge if that pair is not already in the graph. An alternative approach constructs the set of $\binom{n}{2}$ possible edges and selects a random \textit{m}-subset of them, as discussed in Section 17.5 (page 521).
\item \textit{Preferential attachment} -- Under a rich-get-richer model, newly created edges are more likely to point to high-degree vertices than low-degree ones. Consider new links (edges) being added to the graph of webpages. Under any realistic web generation model, it is much more likely the next link will point to Google than www.algorist.com\footnotemark. Selecting the next neighbor with probability proportional to its degree yields graphs with \textit{power law} properties encountered in many real networks.
\end{itemize}

Which of these options best models your application? Probably none of them. By definition, random graphs have very little concrete structure. But graphs are used to model relationships, which are often highly structured. Experiments conducted on random graphs, although interesting and easy to perform, often fail to capture the phenomenon that you are looking for.

An alternative to random graphs is ``organic'' graphs---graphs that reflect the relationships among real-world objects. Many raw sources of relationships are available on the web that can be turned into interesting organic graphs with a little programming and imagination. Consider the graph defined by a set of webpages, with any hyperlink between two pages defining an edge. Or, what about the graph implicit in railroad, subway, or airline networks, with vertices being stations and edges between two stations connected by direct service?

Two classes of graphs have particularly interesting generation algorithms:

\footnotetext{Please link to us from your homepage to correct this travesty.}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Trees} -- Pr\"ufer codes provide a simple way to rank and unrank labeled trees and thus solve all the standard generation problems (see Section 17.4 (page 517)). There are exactly $n^{n-2}$ labeled trees on $n$ vertices, and exactly that many strings of length $n-2$ on the alphabet $\{1,2, \ldots, n\}$.

The key to Pr\"ufer's bijection is the observation that every tree has at least two vertices of degree 1. Thus, in any labeled tree the vertex $v$ incident on the leaf with lowest label is well defined. We take $v$ to be $S_{1}$, the first character in the code. We then delete the associated leaf and repeat the procedure until only two vertices are left. This defines a unique code $S$ for any given labeled tree that can be used to rank the tree. To go from code to tree, observe that the lowest-labeled leaf will be the smallest integer missing from $S$, which when paired with $S_{1}$ determines the first edge of the tree. The entire tree follows by induction.

Algorithms for efficiently generating unlabeled rooted trees are discussed in the Implementations section.

\item \textbf{Fixed degree sequence graphs} -- The degree sequence of a graph $G$ is an integer partition $p=(p_{1}, \ldots, p_{n})$, where $p_{i}$ is the degree of the $i$th highest-degree vertex of $G$. Each edge contributes to the degree of two vertices, so $p$ is an integer partition of $2m$, where $m$ is the number of edges.

Not all partitions correspond to degree sequences of graphs. However, there is a recursive procedure that constructs a graph with a given degree sequence if one exists. If a partition is realizable, the highest-degree vertex $v_{1}$ can be connected to the next $p_{1}$ highest-degree vertices in $G$, or the vertices corresponding to parts $p_{2}, \ldots, p_{p_{1}+1}$. Deleting $p_{1}$ and decrementing $p_{2}, \ldots, p_{p_{1}+1}$ yields a smaller partition to recur on. The partition will be realized if we terminate without ever creating negative numbers. Since we always connect the highest-degree vertex to other high-degree vertices, it is important to reorder the parts of the partition by size after each iteration.
\end{itemize}

