\textit{8.3. SHORTEST PATHS} \hfill 263

\begin{verbatim}
void floyd(adjacency_matrix *g) {
    int i, j;    /* dimension counters */
    int k;       /* intermediate vertex counter */
    int through_k; /* distance through vertex k */
    for (k = 1; k <= g->nvertices; k++) {
        for (i = 1; i <= g->nvertices; i++) {
            for (j = 1; j <= g->nvertices; j++) {
                through_k = g->weight[i][k] + g->weight[k][j];
                if (through_k < g->weight[i][j]) {
                    g->weight[i][j] = through_k;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
\end{verbatim}

The Floyd–Warshall all-pairs shortest-path algorithm runs in $\mathit{O}(n^{3})$ time, which is asymptotically no better than $\mathit{n}$ calls to Dijkstra's algorithm. However, the loops are so tight and the program so short that it runs better in practice. It is notable as one of the rare graph algorithms that work better on adjacency matrices than adjacency lists.

The output of Floyd's algorithm, as it is written, does not enable one to reconstruct the actual shortest path between any given pair of vertices. These paths can be recovered if we retain a parent matrix $\mathit{P}$ containing our choice of the last intermediate vertex used for each vertex pair $(\mathit{x}, \mathit{y})$. Say this value is $\mathit{k}$. The shortest path from $\mathit{x}$ to $\mathit{y}$ is the concatenation of the shortest path from $\mathit{x}$ to $\mathit{k}$ with the shortest path from $\mathit{k}$ to $\mathit{y}$, which can be reconstructed recursively given the matrix $\mathit{P}$. Note, however, that most all-pairs applications only need the resulting distance matrix. These are the jobs that Floyd's algorithm was designed for.

\subsection{Transitive Closure}

Floyd's algorithm has another important application, that of computing \textit{transitive closure}. We are often interested in which vertices in a directed graph are reachable from a given node. As an example, consider the \textit{blackmail graph}, where there is a directed edge $(\mathit{i}, \mathit{j})$ if person $\mathit{i}$ has sensitive-enough private information on person $\mathit{j}$ so that $\mathit{i}$ can get $\mathit{j}$ to do whatever they want. You wish to hire one of these $\mathit{n}$ people to be your personal representative. Who has the most power in terms of blackmail potential?

A simplistic answer would be the vertex of highest out-degree, but an even better representative would be the person who has blackmail chains leading to the most other parties. Steve might only be able to blackmail Miguel directly, but if Miguel can blackmail everyone else then Steve is the person you want to hire.

