& V(i, j) = \max\big(E(i, j), F(i, j), G(i, j)\big) \\
& G(i, j) = V(i-1, j-1) + \textit{match}(i, j) \\
& E(i, j) = \max\big(E(i, j-1), V(i, j-1) - \textit{A}\big) - \textit{B} \\
& F(i, j) = \max\big(F(i-1, j), V(i-1, j) - \textit{A}\big) - \textit{B}
\end{aligned}
\]
With a constant amount of work per cell, this algorithm takes $O(mn)$ time, same as without gap costs.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Does similarity mean strings that sound alike?}
\end{itemize}
Other models of approximate pattern matching become appropriate in certain applications. Particularly interesting is \textit{Soundex}, a hashing scheme that attempts to pair up English words that sound alike. This can be useful in testing whether two names that have been spelled differently are likely to be the same. For example, my last name has been spelled ``Skina,'' ``Skinnia,'' ``Schiena,'' and occasionally ``Skiena.'' All of these hash to the same Soundex code, \textit{S25}.

Soundex drops vowels and silent letters, removes doubled letters, and then assigns the remaining letters numbers from the following classes: \textit{BFPV} gets a 1, \textit{CGJKQSXZ} gets a 2, \textit{DT} gets a 3, \textit{L} gets a 4, \textit{MN} gets a 5, and \textit{R} gets a 6. The characters \textit{HWY} are not assigned a digit. The code starts with the first letter of the name and contains at most three digits. Although this sounds fairly hokey, experience shows that it works reasonably well. Experience indeed: Soundex has been used since the 1920's.

\textbf{Implementations}: Several excellent software tools are available for approximate pattern matching. Manber and Wu's \textit{agrep} [WM92a, WM92b] (approximate general regular expression pattern matcher) is a tool supporting text search with spelling errors. The current version is available at http://www.tgries.de/agrep/. Navarro's \textit{nrgrep} [Nav01b] combines bit-parallelism and filtration, resulting in running times that are more constant than \textit{agrep}, although not always faster. It is available at https://www.dcc.uchile.cl/\textasciitilde gnavarro/software/. \textit{TRE} is a general regular-expression matching library for exact and approximate matching, which is more general than \textit{agrep}. The worst-case complexity is $O(nm^{2})$, where $m$ is the list of the regular expressions involved. \textit{TRE} is available at https://github.com/laurikari/tre/.

