\subsection{Arithmetic}
The use of 64-bit arithmetic is very similar to 32-bit arithmetic except much larger numbers can be used. Whereas there is very little difference with addition and subtraction, there are a few differences with multiplication and division.

Recall from Chap. 3 that when two 32-bit numbers are multiplied the result occupied 64 bits in the \texttt{edx:eax} register pair. A similar result occurs when two 64-bit numbers are multiplied where the results are placed in the 128 bits of the \texttt{rdx:rax} register pair. Consider the following code example:

\begin{verbatim}
mov rax,2147483647
mov rbx,2
imul rbx
\end{verbatim}

The above number loaded into the \texttt{rax} number is the largest signed positive number that can be stored in 32 bits. If 32-bit multiplication were to be used the results would have extended into the \texttt{edx} register. However, when using 64-bit registers, the resulting product can be contained in the 64-bit \texttt{rax} register and only the sign-bit would be propagated into the \texttt{rdx} register. If the number were much larger than this, then it too would extend into the \texttt{rdx} register, but in this text, 64 bits should be more than sufficient.

Similar to 32-bit division where the \texttt{edx:eax} register pair contains the 64-bit dividend, 64-bit division needs to have the dividend initially in the 128-bit \texttt{rdx:}\
\texttt{rax} register pair. Instead of using the \texttt{cdq} (convert double to quad) instruction prior to the 64-bit division, one must use the \texttt{cqo} (convert quad to oct, where oct refers to eight words) instruction. This extends the sign bit of rax into rdx prior to 64-bit division as illustrated in the following code segment:

