\subsection{Logic}

Also as discussed in Sect.\ 11.1, two alternatives of moving 32 bits into a 64-bit register resulted in either the upper-32 bits becoming all zeros or extending the sign bit into the upper 32 bits. What if one did not want to alter the upper 32 bits of a 64-bit register?

The way this can be accomplished is by using various logic instructions. This would involve clearing out the lower 32 bits of the 64-bit register to zero, creating a mask to not alter the upper 32 bits, and using an \texttt{and} instruction to put the new data into the lower 32 bits. Again, assume the data in the \texttt{rax} register is as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
rax
0123456789ABCDEF
\end{verbatim}

The mask necessary to clear out the lower 32 bits yet maintain the upper 32 bits is as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
FFFFFFFF00000000
\end{verbatim}

At first, one might be tempted to put the above in an operand of an \texttt{and} instruction, but it is too large. Instead it can be placed into a memory location using an unsigned \texttt{qword}. Remember when using hexadecimal number that the left most position of the number must be a digit and not a letter, so note the leading 0 as follows:\\
\texttt{mask64 qword 0FFFFFFFF00000000h}\\
Then an \texttt{and} instruction can be used,

\begin{verbatim}
and rax,mask64
\end{verbatim}

which results in the following:

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[max width=\textwidth]{2025_03_24_ebe50cc223a6fbc49eecg-260(1)}
\end{figure}

Now if the new data to be put into the lower 32 bits was $AABBCCDD_{h}$, it could be loaded into a 32-bit register such as \texttt{ebx},

\begin{verbatim}
mov ebx,0AABBCCDDh
\end{verbatim}

which would automatically clear out the upper bits of \texttt{rbx} as follows:

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[max width=\textwidth]{2025_03_24_ebe50cc223a6fbc49eecg-260}
\end{figure}

And lastly, the data in \texttt{rbx} could be placed into the lower 32 bits of \texttt{rax} using an \texttt{or} instruction as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
or rax,rbx
\end{verbatim}

which would result in the new data in the lower 32 bits while the original data in the upper 32 bits would remain undisturbed as shown below:

