sum = 0; for(i = 0; i<3; i++) sum += numary[i];

Of course since the for loop is the best choice as used above, the equivalent code in assembly language would be the use of the \texttt{.repeat-.untilcxz} directive. Unlike C code, where the variable $i$ is being used both as a loop counter and as an index, in assembly language, two separate registers need to be used. So in addition to using the \texttt{ebx} register for indexing, the \texttt{ecx} register would be used for loop control. Lastly, since \texttt{sum} was not initialized in the data section, it must be initialized to 0 during execution time. The resulting code is as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
mov sum,0 ; initialize sum to 0
mov ecx,3 ; initialize ecx to 3
mov ebx,0 ; initialize ebx to 0
.repeat
mov eax,numary[ebx] ; load eax with element of numary
add sum,eax ; add eax to sum
add ebx,4 ; increment ebx by 4
.untilcxz
\end{verbatim}

As discussed in the previous section, note that a 4 needs to be added to the \texttt{ebx} register to access the next signed double word. Also, do not forget that the \texttt{ecx} register should not be altered, since the \texttt{.untilcxz} directive decrements the \texttt{ecx} register by 1 automatically. After walking through the above code segment, the contents of the registers and memory locations would be as shown in Fig.~8.4, where all values are in hexadecimal.

Why does the \texttt{ebx} register have the hexadecimal number 0000000C (decimal 12) in it? Could it not end up addressing the memory location \texttt{sum}? Prior to the loop, the \texttt{ebx} register is initialized to 0 to access the first element in the array. After accessing and summing the current element in the array, the value in \texttt{ebx} is incremented by 4 in anticipation of accessing the next element in the array the next time through the loop. To answer the first question, during the last iteration of the loop and after the third memory location has been accessed, \texttt{ebx} is incremented by 4 to 0000000C in anticipation of accessing the next element in the array. The answer to the second question would then be yes, where the memory location \texttt{sum}

