\subsection{Indexing Using the \texttt{esi} and \texttt{edi} Registers}

Although the use of the base register \texttt{ebx} is fairly straightforward, being limited to a single register can prove to be somewhat restrictive. To help with this matter, there are two index registers \texttt{esi} and \texttt{edi}, where the \texttt{esi} register is known as the source index register and \texttt{edi} is known as the destination index register. The subtle difference between the use of the \texttt{ebx} register and the \texttt{esi} and \texttt{edi} registers is that the former is used with the name of the array as an index and the latter are used more like pointers. In this latter case, the address of the array is first loaded into the register and the name of the array is not subsequently used. Also, these two registers are very useful when manipulating strings, as will be discussed in the next chapter.

To demonstrate the different way of addressing using these registers, it is easier to examine the addressing of only a single element of an array first. For example, if only the second element of an array needed to be moved into the \texttt{eax} register, the instruction \texttt{mov eax, numary +4} could be written as done in the first section of this chapter. However, could the contents of \texttt{numary+4} be moved into \texttt{eax} without using +4 attached to the name of the array? Yes, the same thing could be accomplished by using a register as an index. The following code segment would work the same assuming the existence of the previous array, \texttt{numary}:

\begin{verbatim}
mov ebx,4
mov eax,numary[ebx]
\end{verbatim}

Although a bit cumbersome compared to using \texttt{numary +4}, this code segment should make sense given the previous section, where \texttt{ebx} contains a 4, and \texttt{numary} indexed by \texttt{ebx} would be the address of the second element of \texttt{numary}. The dashed arrows in Fig.~8.5 show how the address of \texttt{numary}, which is 100, is added to or indexed by the 4 in the \texttt{ebx} register to create the address 104, which is the address of the second element of the array \texttt{numary}. The solid arrow in Fig.~8.5 shows the contents of memory location 104 being copied into the \texttt{eax} register.

Using the \texttt{esi} register could accomplish the same task in the following code segment which introduces the new \texttt{offset} operator:

\begin{verbatim}
mov esi,offset numary+4
mov eax,[esi]
\end{verbatim}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[max width=\textwidth]{2025_03_24_ebe50cc223a6fbc49eecg-180}
    \caption{Using \texttt{ebx} register to access a single element}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[max width=\textwidth]{2025_03_24_ebe50cc223a6fbc49eecg-181}
    \caption{Using \texttt{esi} to access a single element}
\end{figure}

Instead of causing the ``contents'' of \texttt{numary+4} to be loaded into the \texttt{esi} register, the word \texttt{offset} causes the ``address'' of \texttt{numary+4} to be loaded into the \texttt{esi} register. In the second statement, notice that there is no reference to \texttt{numary} because the address of \texttt{numary +4} was loaded into the \texttt{esi} in the first line. Now the address in \texttt{esi} can be used like a pointer, indicated by the square brackets, to point to the data in the second element in the array, where its contents are transferred to the \texttt{eax} register. The dashed arrow in Fig.~8.6 shows the \texttt{esi} register pointing to the second element of \texttt{numary} at memory location 104 and the solid arrow in Fig.~8.6 shows the contents of memory location 104 being copied into the \texttt{eax} register.

