In this case the results would look the same as when using the \texttt{mov} instruction, although the process is different. In the case of the \texttt{mov} instruction the upper bits are cleared to zero and in the \texttt{movsxd} case the sign-bit is propagated into the upper 32-bits. If the end result is the same, why would one use \texttt{movsxd}? The reason is that if there is unknown data in a 32-bit register or memory location then the \texttt{movsxd} would extend the sign-bit, whether a zero or a one, accordingly. For example, assume the variable \texttt{num32} contains a signed integer:

\begin{verbatim}
mov ebx, num32
movsxd rax,ebx
\end{verbatim}

Then regardless of what is in the sign-bit in \texttt{num32}, it will be extended into the upper 32 bits of \texttt{rax}. This principle also applies to the other three general purpose registers as well: $\mathrm{rbx}$, $\mathrm{rcx}$, and $\mathrm{rdx}$.

Does the default of clearing the upper 32 bits of a 64-bit register cause any difficulties? If one is using just 32 bits in a 64-bit environment, then there is really no concern what is happening in the upper 32 bits of \texttt{rax} or the other registers. And if one is using only 64-bit registers and instructions then again this should be of no concern. Only if one is moving from the 32-bit environment to the 64-bit environment and is accustomed to the upper bits of a register being maintained or if one is mixing 32-bit and 64-bit registers and processing, then it could cause some difficulties. However, provided one is aware of the differences and is careful when choosing the correct instructions such as \texttt{movsxd}, then it should not pose much of a problem.

\subsection{Other 64-Bit Registers}
In addition to the four general purpose registers, other registers also have 64-bit variations. These include the index registers \texttt{esi} and \texttt{edi} which are available as \texttt{rsi} and \texttt{rdi}, and the stack registers \texttt{esp} and \texttt{ebp} as \texttt{rsp} and \texttt{rbp}, respectively. The \texttt{eflags} register also has a 64-bit counterpart as \texttt{rflags}, but although this could allow for more error flag bits in the future, at present the upper 32 bits of this register are not used. Also, the instruction pointer register \texttt{eip} has a corresponding 64-bit register \texttt{rip}.

