\hline
add reg, mem & add the contents of memory to the register \\
\hline
add mem,reg & add the contents of the register to memory \\
\hline
add reg,imm & add immediate value to the register \\
\hline
add reg,reg & \begin{tabular}{l}
add the contents of the source (second) register to the \\
destination (first) register \\
\end{tabular} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

As before, the C statement makes a nice general comment prior to the assembly language code segment. In the above segment, the contents of \texttt{num1} are copied into the \texttt{eax} register, then the contents of \texttt{num2} are added to \texttt{eax}, and lastly the contents of \texttt{eax} are copied into the variable \texttt{sum}. Assuming \texttt{num1} initially contains a 5 and \texttt{num2} contains a 7, then Fig. 3.1 shows the results after execution of the previous code segment, where \texttt{eax} and \texttt{sum} shown in green contain a 12.

Although it is possible to use any of the other three registers and accomplish the same task, it is usually better to use the accumulator, the \texttt{eax} register, because the arithmetic instructions that use the \texttt{eax} register tend to use less memory and are also a little faster, as will be seen in Chap. 12. Also, just like there are often many ways to solve a problem in high-level languages, the same is true in low-level languages. Further, just like some solutions are better solutions in high-level languages, the same is also true in low-level languages. For example, the previous assembly code segment could have been written as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
mov sum,0 ; initialize sum to zero
mov eax, num1 ; load eax with the contents of num1
add sum, eax ; add the contents of eax to sum
mov eax, num2 ; load eax with the contents of num2
add sum, eax ; add eax to sum
\end{verbatim}

