The enqueue routine should look somewhat familiar to those who have taken a second semester computer science course in data structures. Although there can be some complex ways to determine whether a queue is full, the simplest method is to use a counter such as \textit{count}, which is checked to see if it is less than the length of the queue, $n$ in this case. If so, \textit{number} is placed in the rear of the \textit{queue}. The variable \textit{rear} is incremented by $1$. The mod function (\%) is used to cause \textit{rear} to be reset to $0$ should it exceed the length of the queue ($n$). For example, if \textit{rear} is equal to $3$, the $3 \% 3$ is equal to $0$. The \textit{dequeue} routine is similar and it is left for the reader to walk through the procedure:

