One nice way to turn \textit{n} points in \textit{d} dimensions into \textit{n} points in \textit{n} dimensions might be to represent each point by its distances to all \textit{n} input points. In particular, for each point $p_{i}$ we can create a vector $v_{i}$ such that $v_{ij} = \operatorname{dist}(i, j)$, the distance from $p_{i}$ to $p_{j}$. The vector of such distances should serve as a powerful set of features for classifying any new point $q$, since the distances to members of the actual class should be small compared to those of the other class.

