\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Matrix multiplication is associative}: \textit{Associativity} grants us the right to parenthesize as we wish, performing operations in the relative order that we choose. In computing the product $ABC$, we have a choice of two options: $(AB)C$ or $A(BC)$. Longer chains of matrices permit even more freedom, with the number of possible parenthesizations growing exponentially in the length of the chain. All of these will return the same answer, as demonstrated here:
\end{itemize}

$$
