\subsection{Matrix Multiplication}
Matrix multiplication is an aggregate version of the vector \textit{dot} or \textit{inner product}. Recall that for two $n$-element vectors, $\mathit{X}$ and $\mathit{Y}$, the dot product $\mathit{X} \cdot \mathit{Y}$ is defined:
\[
\mathit{X} \cdot \mathit{Y} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathit{X}_i \mathit{Y}_i
\]
Dot products measure how ``in sync'' the two vectors are. We have already seen the dot product when computing the cosine distance and correlation coefficient. It is an operation that reduces a pair of vectors to a single number.

The matrix product $\mathit{X} \mathit{Y}^{\mathit{T}}$ of these two vectors produces a $1 \times 1$ matrix containing the dot product $\mathit{X} \cdot \mathit{Y}$. For general matrices, the product $\mathit{C} = \mathit{A} \mathit{B}$ is defined by:
\[
\mathit{C}_{ij} = \sum_{k=1}^{k} \mathit{A}_{ik} \cdot \mathit{B}_{kj}
\]
For this to work, $\mathit{A}$ and $\mathit{B}$ must share the same inner dimensions, implying that if $\mathit{A}$ is $n \times k$ then $\mathit{B}$ must have dimensions $k \times m$. Each element of the $n \times m$ product matrix $\mathit{C}$ is a dot product of the $i$th row of $\mathit{A}$ with the $j$th column of $\mathit{B}$.

The most important properties of matrix multiplication are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{It does not commute}: \textit{Commutativity} is the notation that order doesn't matter, that $\mathit{x} \cdot \mathit{y} = \mathit{y} \cdot \mathit{x}$. Although we take commutativity for granted when multiplying integers, \textit{order does matter} in matrix multiplication. For any pair of non-square matrices $\mathit{A}$ and $\mathit{B}$, at most one of either $\mathit{A}\mathit{B}$ or $\mathit{B}\mathit{A}$ has compatible dimensions. But even square matrix multiplication does not commute, as shown by the products below:
\end{itemize}

